Z||000004||kempo||10-17-2001||03:47 PM||||Formula- I don't understand tire sizes.  What do the numbers and letters mean?  We'll use stock fiero tire sizes.<BR>P205/60R15 - Front<BR>P215/60R15 - Rear<P>What do the numbers mean?  Wish I knew more about tires.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||10-17-2001||04:10 PM||||205=width of tire  60=ratio of width to sidewall height.<P>So on the front of your car the dia 24.69 &  25.16 on the back.<P>If you want 17 in rims...look at the bottom 2 boxes of my chart.  If you want to keep the diameters the same, find similar #s on the bottom box(total diameter).<P>Well we have a 24.62--look left 215, look up 45....so you may want a 215/45R17<P>In back.---maybe a 24.97?  225/45R17?<P><BR>The top box is a conversion to inches W"=width in inches...and the sidewall height.<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 10-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||kempo||10-17-2001||04:46 PM||||Joe- I think I'm beginning to understand you.  Would these sizes fit my fiero good -&gt;without&lt;- raising the height of it?<BR>Front- 215/45R17<BR>Rear - 225/45R17<P>These are your measurements.  I also don't want to have the tires sticking out the sides like some people do (as on Honda's and such).  I don't think that these sizes would.  Thanks for all your help!<P>Edit- bad analogy, my appologies.<p>[This message has been edited by kempo (edited 10-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Philphine||10-17-2001||05:07 PM||||use a 50 series tire for example. it's easier. 225/50-16. the 50 means the tire is 50% as tall as it is wide. so if you measure across the bulge of the tire (about half way bettween the rim and the tread) it would measure 225 millimeters (theoretically. rim width and tire brand will make this vary). so if you measure the tire from the rim strait out to the tread it should be 112.5mm (again, theoretically). 50% of the section width.<P>hmm...when you try to write it out it looks confusing even though i wrote it myself. hope it makes sence and helps. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000008||kempo||10-17-2001||05:31 PM||||It's all so very confusing to me.  I don't understand how you come up with the ratio number, the 50 in your 225/50-16.  It works, as it's almost exact with the tire calculator that Formula linked me to.  I think that the 215/45R17 and 225/45R17 are my best selections to use after playing around with the tire calculator for a long time.  Still much to learn...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif">||||reg||1
