Z||000010||kempo||10-17-2001||05:43 PM||||Could I use the same tire size for front and rear?  Just for tire purchasing's sake.  <BR>Say<BR>215/45R17 on both front and rear?  hmmm...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||jelly2m8||10-17-2001||06:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kempo:<BR><B>Could I use the same tire size for front and rear?  Just for tire purchasing's sake.  <BR>Say<BR>215/45R17 on both front and rear?  hmmm...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR> Sure you can. I run 225/50 15's all the way around my 87, I love it. I drove a customers 87 with the stock 205 / 215 combo the other day, and I couldn't push it as hard in the corners as I could mine.<P> Plus you will have the benefit of being able to rotate your tires, resulting in longer tire wear. ( if the front and rear rims have the same offset)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Joe Torma||10-17-2001||06:25 PM||||LOOKIE  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">....I put more rim sizes on the chart!<P>Anyway...Yes, Kempo you can use different sizes...and the sizes you picked won't increase the height.  Actually they are slightly shorter.<P>What the sidewall number is, is a percent of the width.  If you have a 60 series tire, the sidewall is 60% of the width.<P>I have 225/45/17 Rear and 215/45/16 fronts.  I will be upping the size a bit next time 235 & 225.<P>I used to have the 225/50/15s all the way around...a bit small(big wheel gap) but a great size--I liked them.<P>Does this help any?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">||||reg||10
