Z||000015||Rick 88||10-17-2001||08:00 PM||||kempo,<P>Bad analogy like you said. No offense taken.<P>The 215/45/17's might look a little small on the rear. If you car is not an 88, and you want to rotate tires the 225/45/17's will fill the openings more and should not have any clearance problems in front.<P>Rick 88<BR>on the border with Mexico<P>||||reg||14
Z||000016||Philphine||10-17-2001||08:07 PM||||i just used a 50 series 'cause it's easy to figure out for example. exactly 1/2. try this then. same size 225/50-16.<BR>225= section width like explained before.<BR>50= aspect ratio. how tall the tire is in relation to how wide it is.<BR>16= the rim it fits on.<P>225x.03937= the section width in inches. 8.85"<P>8.85xthe aspect ratio (50%)=the height of the tire from the rim to the thread. 4.43"<P>4.43x2=because there is tire above and below the rim. 8.85 (notice with a 50% ratio you just end up with your original number again. you need these steps more with other ratios).<P>last, 8.85+the rim size, 16=24.85<P>what ever size you're considering, just plug in the numbers and you get the size (brand and rim width alter this some). i play with cars and truck ideas constantly so the formulas in my head (still need a calculator to actually do it).<P>one more. 215/45-17<BR>215x.03937=8.46"<BR>8.46"x45%=3.80"<BR>3.80"x2=7.61"<BR>7.61"+17"=24.61"<P>any clearer?||||reg||1
Z||000017||sqoach||10-17-2001||09:42 PM||||I kinda know about how the numbers work. But it still confuses me. I went into a Pep Boys a while back, and saw a 205 50-16 tire, and it was wide! Not super wide, but wider than I thought it would be. Looks much wider than the 205 60-14 on my SE. They are different brands, but still, there was quite a bit of difference. Why is that?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">              <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Formula88||10-18-2001||10:55 AM||||Some tires of the same size are wider than other due to how the manufacturer makes the tire.  If the tread is more square and blocked off from side to side, it will appear wider than one with a more rounded tread, even though the measured width is the same.  ( I think I just confused myself... )<P>BFG TA's ahve a squareish shoulder where most generic tires are more rounded from the tread to the sidewall.  That would make the BFG TA look wider than the generic tire, even if they were the exact same size.  ||||reg||6
Z||000019||kempo||10-21-2001||07:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rick 88:<BR><B>The 215/45/17's might look a little small on the rear. If you car is not an 88, and you want to rotate tires the 225/45/17's will fill the openings more and should not have any clearance problems in front.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was told that the size 225/45/17 may rub against the fender well, and that I should probably stick with 215's all the way around.  My plan was to use the 225/45/17's on all four.  I don't want to rub against the fender, so what's the best way to go?||||reg||1
